The legal code is the licence; it is linked here, not copied. In short (the legal code governs):
  - Attribution: credit the creator, link the licence and the source, and say what was changed. The header at the top
    of each file and this file do that; keep them.
  - NonCommercial: not for commercial purposes.
  - ShareAlike: adaptations of these files are shared under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 in turn, so they stay non-commercial.

Changes
-------
Changes made by moodreel (2026-11-08):

  look.js, post.js, paint.js, util.js
    - three.js is taken from moodreel's kit/three.js (import { THREE } from '../three.js') instead of the bare 'three'
      import, so a film needs no import map and shares one three.js with the rest of the kit;
    - a header at the top naming the source, the licence and the changes.
    Nothing else: below the header the code is the source's, line for line.

  text.js
    - the same import change and header;
    - the default title lines of titleTexture (now ['TITLE']) and the default phrase of createWriter (now 'HELLO'),
      which quoted a film's on-screen words, made neutral, and the comments that quoted them reworded.

  stage.js
    - film(), an adapter for moodreel's stage, replaces the page boot and its query-string shot loader: several shots
      on one page, each with its own ctx, scene and camera, sharing one renderer and one Post per supersampling
      factor; moodreel's render(t) is synchronous, so a shot's render that returns a Promise is an error;
    - the renderer draws on an offscreen canvas that is never added to the page (the stage's 2D canvas stays the
      page's only canvas);
    - the global state a shot's init leaves (the look env's uniform values; the renderer's tone mapping and exposure,
      shadow map, clear colour and alpha, output colour space, clipping, viewport and scissor) is kept and put back
      before each of its frames, and every shot's init starts from the state before the first one, so no frame
      depends on what was rendered or initialised before it;
    - loadGLTF (it needs three.js's add-ons through an import map) and the imports of the showcase's film-specific
      modules (props, heroine, kit) removed; a film hands its own modules to every ctx as extras;
    - window.__ctx and the gl.finish() after each frame dropped; the import change; the original header reworded;
      a header at the top naming the source, the licence and the changes.
    Kept from the source: setupShadows, keyLight, loadTexture, the ctx with its helpers (draw through Post,
    renderToTexture, time / local / progress, rng, track, spline, cameraRig and the rest of util), the hold rule and
    the page's query flags (hold, ss, nopost, post).

  README.md
    - written by moodreel to describe this directory; shared under the same licence.

The rest of moodreel
--------------------
Everything outside kit/3dcv is moodreel's own work under the MIT licence (LICENSE at the repository root) and does not
include this code. kit/look3d.js and kit/post3d.js, for instance, are moodreel's own MIT modules, written from ideas
only. Code from kit/3dcv is not to be copied into MIT files. A film that uses kit/3dcv carries these files, with this
licence, in its own kit/3dcv folder; the licence goes on applying to them there.

No warranties
-------------
As the licence says, the work is offered as-is and as-available, without representations or warranties of any kind;
the same holds for moodreel's changes.
